|           2       2                                                    |
|o22 = - 72x x  + 3x  + 8x x  + x                                        |
|           3 4     3     3 4    3                                       |
|                                                                        |
|                      ZZ[x ..x ]                                        |
|                          0   4                                         |
|o22 : -----------------------------------------                         |
|      (x x , x x x , x  - x , x  - x , x  - x )                         |
|        2 4   0 1 3   0    3   1    3   2    4                          |
+------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i23 : Ch=ToricChowRing(X)                                               |
|                                                                        |
|o23 = Ch                                                                |
|                                                                        |
|o23 : QuotientRing                                                      |
+------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i24 : s3=Segre(Ch,X,I)                                                  |
|                                                                        |
|           2       2                                                    |
|o24 = - 72x x  + 3x  + 8x x  + x                                        |
|           3 4     3     3 4    3                                       |
|                                                                        |
|o24 : Ch                                                                |
+------------------------------------------------------------------------+

All the examples were done using symbolic computations with Gr\"obner bases.
Changing the option *note CompMethod: CompMethod, to bertini will do the main
computations numerically, provided Bertini is  *note installed and configured:
configuring Bertini,. Note that the bertini option is only available for
subschemes of \PP^n.

Observe that the algorithm is a probabilistic algorithm and may give a wrong
answer with a small but nonzero probability. Read more under *note
probabilistic algorithm: probabilistic algorithm,.

Ways to use Segre:
==================

  * "Segre(Ideal)"
  * "Segre(Ideal,Symbol)"
  * "Segre(QuotientRing,Ideal)"

